    {
      type: "book",
      title: "Accelerate",
      author: "Nicole Forsgren, Jez Humble, and Gene Kim",
      display_author: "Nicole Forsgren et al.",
      url: "https://openlibrary.org/books/OL26833682M/Accelerate_The_Science_of_Lean_Software_and_DevOps",
      added: "2026-08-09T06:45:00Z",
      excerpts: [
        "The ability to work and deliver in small batches is especially important, because it allows you to gather user feedback quickly.",
      ],
      published: "2018",
    },
    {
      type: "talk",
      title: "Leadership Without Management",
      author: "Bryan Cantrill",
      url: "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1KeYzjILqDo",
      added: "2026-08-18T23:30:00Z",
      excerpts: [
        "The primary motivator for superlative engineers is mission, purpose. If you can’t explain why your company exists, don’t expect to ever find superlative talent. Ever. Doesn’t matter how much money you have. Compensation is the fourth most important thing. No higher, no lower.",
        "The second you allow the numbers to be formalized, you’re going to get organizational jockeying, and then you ultimately have the mother of all perverse incentives: you are actually incentivized to not work with good people. Ranking is organizational cancer. Do not do it under any conditions.",
        "Functionality, quality, and schedule: you get to pick only two. If you’re going to pick functionality and schedule, I’m out of here, and so is every other high quality engineer, because I don’t want to deliver shit.",
      ],
      published: "2013-09-13",
    },
    {
      type: "talk",
      title: "Thinking Above the Code",
      author: "Leslie Lamport",
      url: "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-4Yp3j_jk8Q",
      added: "2026-08-18T23:31:00Z",
      excerpts: [
        "Writing is nature’s way of letting you know how sloppy your thinking is. To think, to really think, you have to write. If you’re thinking without writing, chances are you’re fooling yourself. We’re only pretending to think.",
        "When they’re telling you not to write a spec, they’re really telling you not to think. Examples are great to explain, but they’re terrible. They keep you from thinking. You think in terms of these particular examples. The real problem in programming is not to think of the easy cases. The hard cases. And you don’t get that with examples.",
      ],
      published: "2014-07-14",
    },
    {
      type: "talk",
      title: "SQLite and Go",
      author: "David Crawshaw",
      url: "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RqubKSF3wig",
      added: "2026-08-18T23:32:00Z",
      excerpts: [
        "When you’re at a big tech company and you have a bunch of engineering problems, if you can’t solve them you just go and find more engineers. If you have a lot of money and you have a big problem, you use the money to solve the problem. It’s completely reasonable. But it doesn’t work for me, because I don’t have all the money, so I have to just solve it myself.",
        "I write a server in a process. I can scale my service until I hit the limits of one computer, and then it’s all over. Fortunately I don’t need to build big distributed things. It turns out one computer can do an awful lot. A lot of the moderately large services I worked at Google could have been built this way.",
      ],
      published: "2018-07-30",
    },
    {
      type: "link",
      title: "The Revolt of the Reader",
      author: "Bryan Cantrill",
      url: "https://bcantrill.dtrace.org/2026/09/05/the-revolt-of-the-reader/",
      added: "2026-09-06T06:01:16Z",
      excerpts: [
        "To those who read broadly, the hand of the LLM is so clear it’s as if the writer’s intellectual fly is open.",
      ],
      published: "2026-09-05",
    },
